1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,895
Olá. This is Liam, in for Bear, with INFO 7375, Computational Skepticism for AI, at

2
00:00:04,915 --> 00:00:09,155
Northeastern. This film is about Gru, a persona that conducts Claude through a

3
00:00:09,175 --> 00:00:14,398
software design. Gru runs a design in five phases, and no phase starts until the one

4
00:00:14,418 --> 00:00:18,986
before it is validated. You'll watch that rule catch a component nobody asked for.

5
00:00:19,006 --> 00:00:22,590
The trap: a document with words in every section looks finished.

6
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:27,693
Why should you care? Ask Claude for a design and it will write a polished one, even

7
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:32,151
for the wrong problem. In Gru's telling, the outage doesn't come from bad code. It

8
00:00:32,171 --> 00:00:36,312
comes from a decision nobody made explicitly. Vague needs upstream turn into mush in

9
00:00:36,332 --> 00:00:40,176
every section downstream. The gates force each decision into the open before Claude

10
00:00:40,196 --> 00:00:40,770
starts typing.

11
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,378
The real question: how does Gru stop Claude from writing the wrong design? Not how

12
00:00:45,398 --> 00:00:49,936
it writes one faster. Gru says it outright: I'm not here to build your rocket

13
00:00:49,956 --> 00:00:50,240
faster.

14
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:54,779
Four terms. An SDD: a software design document, the written record of what a system

15
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:59,268
does, and why. A need: a user or business requirement that a component exists to

16
00:00:59,288 --> 00:01:03,757
serve. A component: a named part of the system with its own job. And falsifiable:

17
00:01:03,777 --> 00:01:06,750
stated so that it could turn out to be wrong.

18
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:10,912
Here's a design document for a checkout page. Five sections: the problem, the needs,

19
00:01:10,932 --> 00:01:14,731
the principles, the components, and the assembled design. Every one of them has

20
00:01:14,751 --> 00:01:19,137
words in it. Two months later, at two in the morning, checkout breaks inside a

21
00:01:19,157 --> 00:01:22,661
recommendation widget. The on-call engineer opens the document and asks one thing:

22
00:01:22,681 --> 00:01:26,480
why does this exist? Every section has words. None of them says why.

23
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:31,165
Gru's fourth phase has one rule: every component has to trace to a need from phase

24
00:01:31,185 --> 00:01:35,220
two. Take the need Gru itself offers as a good one: reduce checkout abandonment

25
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:38,697
caused by the three-step address form. Now propose a component, a single-page

26
00:01:38,717 --> 00:01:43,042
address form. Draw its line back. It lands. That component has a reason to exist,

27
00:01:43,062 --> 00:01:44,780
and the reason is written down.

28
00:01:44,850 --> 00:01:48,761
Vary one thing. Someone says, we might as well add recommendations. Propose a

29
00:01:48,781 --> 00:01:53,297
recommendation widget, and try to draw its line back. Nothing in phase two is about

30
00:01:53,317 --> 00:01:57,531
recommendations. The line has nowhere to land. Gru stops right here and asks which

31
00:01:57,551 --> 00:01:59,950
need it serves, or calls it scope creep.

32
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:04,191
Now the obvious approach. Skip the phases: ask Claude for the whole design in one

33
00:02:04,211 --> 00:02:08,662
pass, then review it at the end. All five sections fill at once, and the needs

34
00:02:08,682 --> 00:02:12,294
section says what first drafts often say: improve user experience. Watch what that

35
00:02:12,314 --> 00:02:16,765
does. The address form traces to it. So does the widget. Everything traces to a need

36
00:02:16,785 --> 00:02:19,280
that vague. Every check passes, and the widget ships.

37
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:24,060
Change the picture. Put a gate in front of every phase. Gru's rule: you cannot get a

38
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:27,954
valid output from the next phase until this one has been validated. Not attempted.

39
00:02:27,974 --> 00:02:31,849
Not mentioned earlier in the chat. Validated. The problem passes. Now the needs gate

40
00:02:31,869 --> 00:02:36,023
asks its one question: could this need turn out to be wrong? Improve user experience

41
00:02:36,043 --> 00:02:37,970
can't be wrong. It fails the gate.

42
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:42,464
Before it moves, commit. Gate two is about to force that need into something

43
00:02:42,484 --> 00:02:46,590
specific. When it does, where does the widget's line land? Pick your answer.

44
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:51,090
It doesn't land anywhere. The need becomes specific, and the fog it sat in collapses

45
00:02:51,110 --> 00:02:55,243
to one point. The address form's line finds it. The widget's line finds nothing.

46
00:02:55,263 --> 00:02:59,693
Gate four holds, and the widget goes back with a question instead of into the

47
00:02:59,713 --> 00:02:59,990
design.

48
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:05,132
This deserves a name. A phase gate: no valid output from the next phase until this

49
00:03:05,152 --> 00:03:09,587
one is validated, not just attempted. Skip one, and Gru names exactly what's missing

50
00:03:09,607 --> 00:03:14,042
and holds the line. Because a decision made without its prerequisite is, in Gru's

51
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:16,270
words, a guess wearing a decision's clothes.

52
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:20,134
Back to two in the morning. Checkout breaks, but not inside a recommendation widget,

53
00:03:20,154 --> 00:03:24,220
because there isn't one. The engineer points at the address form and asks the same

54
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:28,306
question: why does this exist? Follow the line. The answer is sitting in phase two,

55
00:03:28,326 --> 00:03:31,030
in words that could have turned out to be wrong.

56
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:35,558
Two things this film didn't cover. Silent mode skips every gate, for when you

57
00:03:35,578 --> 00:03:39,715
already know exactly what you want and need output, not supervision. And slash

58
00:03:39,735 --> 00:03:43,553
claude, the boondoggle score, ignores the gates on purpose: it scores whatever

59
00:03:43,573 --> 00:03:47,391
exists, at any stage, across the five supervisory capacities. Neither is the

60
00:03:47,411 --> 00:03:49,310
default. The gates are the point.

61
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:53,396
Let's recap with Claude. Gru conducts Claude through five phases: the problem, the

62
00:03:53,416 --> 00:03:57,121
needs, the principles, the components, and the assembled design. Each phase is

63
00:03:57,141 --> 00:04:01,467
gated: no valid output from the next phase until this one is validated, not

64
00:04:01,487 --> 00:04:06,124
attempted. Needs have to be falsifiable, and every component has to trace to one, or

65
00:04:06,144 --> 00:04:10,160
it's scope creep. And a finished-looking document proves only that every section has

66
00:04:10,180 --> 00:04:10,470
words.

67
00:04:10,540 --> 00:04:14,389
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Act as Gru, a phase-gated software architect. My

68
00:04:14,409 --> 00:04:17,981
system, in one sentence: [describe something you are building]. Run only the first

69
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:21,849
two phases. First, restate my problem so that it could not describe ten other

70
00:04:21,869 --> 00:04:26,271
systems. Then list my needs, and reject any need that could not turn out to be

71
00:04:26,291 --> 00:04:30,415
wrong. Do not propose components or write any design yet. Before you run it, predict

72
00:04:30,435 --> 00:04:34,560
which of your needs gets rejected. Then check three things. Did it refuse to design

73
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:38,981
anything before the needs were fixed? Did it reject a need you thought was fine? And

74
00:04:39,001 --> 00:04:42,850
could its problem statement describe ten other systems? If it could, send it back.

75
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:48,310
Gru's Phase Gates: Validated, Not Attempted. At Nik Bear Brown.
